1. d4 d5 2. c4 e5 3. dxe5 d4 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. a3 a5 6. g3 f6 7. exf6 Nxf6 8. Bg2
Bc5 9. O-O O-O 10. Nbd2 Ba7 11. Ne1 Bg4 12. Nd3 Qe7 13. Nf3 Rfe8 14. Re1 Qf7
15. Qc2 Qh5 16. Bf4 Re7 17. b4 Rae8 18. h4 h6 19. b5 Nd8 20. c5 b6 21. Nxd4 (
21. c6 {: surprisingly White misses the chance to be effectively a piece up 
(Andy Ansel)}) 21... bxc5 22. Nf3 Nf7 23. Be3 Bf5 24. Qa2 Bg4 25. Bf4 Be6 26.
Qb2 {From here, the rest of the game score is given in BCM, October 1961, p275,
with Peter Clarke's notes: "The second upset - a real one this time - was
Kottnauer's loss to the young Sussex player Springgay. He is a typical
"natural" player; he has little knowledge of theory or technique but plenty of
talent for tactics; he loves to attack and is not afraid to sacrifice, soundly
or otherwise. Such opponents are among the most dangerous, and Kottnauer ran
into just the sort of situation they revel in. A pawn up with the better game
for a long while, he began to underestimate his opponent's threats, became
confused and short of time and before he knew where he was the complications
had engulfed him. Before the position shown was reached White had already
given some ground, but now all trace of terra firma was swept away as the
black pieces burst into action."} c4 27. Nde5 c3 28. Qc1 g5 29. hxg5 hxg5 30.
Be3 ({If} 30. Nxg5 {then} Nxe5 31. Bxe5 Ng4 {wins}) ({while if} 30. Bxg5 {both}
Ne4 {and 30...Bxf2+ are strong; the text move, however loses a piece.}) 30...
Bxe3 31. Qxe3 Bd5 {The move which Kottnauer had overlooked.} 32. Qxc3 Nxe5 33.
Nxe5 Rxe5 34. Qxc7 Ng4 35. e4 Rf5 $1 {Springgay now winds up the game in fine
style, and it matters little that in the rush he misses a mate in one on move
thirty-nine.} 36. Re2 Ref8 37. f3 ({Or} 37. exf5 Qh2+ 38. Kf1 Qxg2+ 39. Ke1
Qg1+ {, etc.}) 37... Qh2+ 38. Kf1 Rxf3+ 39. Bxf3 Rxf3+ $2 (39... Qh1#) 40. Ke1
Qg1+ 41. Kd2 Qd4+ {Sources: British Championship, Aberystwyth 1961, compiled
by Eric Fisher; BCM, October 1961, p275 (PH Clarke annotations). Contributed
by Andy Ansel, 19 May 2023.} 0-1

