1. c4 c5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. g3 d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. Bg2 Nc7 6. d3 e5 7. f4 exf4 8.
Bxf4 Bd6 $2 9. Qa4+ Kf8 ({Edwards remarks that 9...Kf8 was forced because if} 
9... Bd7 10. Qe4+) 10. Bxd6+ Qxd6 11. Ne4 Qe7 12. Rc1 $16 b6 13. Nf2 $2 ({
13 Nf2 is a poor move, and was played after half an hour’s thought. However,
} 13. Nxc5 $2 bxc5 14. Bxa8 {does not seem to be quite good enough.}) ({
Stockfish 15:} 13. b4 $1 $16) 13... Bd7 14. Qc2 Nc6 15. Nf3 Re8 16. O-O $2 g6
$2 17. Ne4 Kg7 18. Nfd2 $4 (18. Qc3+ f6 19. Nxf6 Qxf6 20. Qxf6+ Kxf6 21. Nd4+
$11) 18... f5 19. Nc3 Nd4 20. Qb1 ({20 Qb1 is a blunder in a lost position. If
} 20. Qd1 {Black would get a winning ending after} Nxe2+ {followed by the
exchange of queens.}) 20... Qe3+ {Source: RW Ives column, Yorkshire Evening
Post 1961. Contributed by Ingrid Ives via the English Chess Forum, 26 April
2023.} 0-1

